  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nA drier and similar hot weather pattern takes shape by Sunday as the \nupper-level ridge retrogrades west near the region. Sunday looks to \nbe the last appreciable day of isolated to scattered storms mainly \nfor areas near/in the Davis Mountains and far West Texas. Ensemble \nguidance is in good agreement with the ridge being centered over the \nforecast area by Monday where sinking motion under this feature \nhinders storm development. Cannot rule out an isolated storm or two \nover the Davis Mountains early next week due to upslope flow, though \nchances are very low (< 10%). High temperatures throughout the \nperiod span in the mid to upper 90s for most locations. Spots along \nthe Rio Grande and Pecos River are forecast to be in the low 100s \neach day (100-108F). By mid to late next week, guidance becomes \nunclear where the upper ridge will be positioned. Some solutions \nsuggest the ridge gets pushed eastward thanks to an upper-level \nstorm system moving across the western CONUS, while other solutions \nsuggest the ridge remaining over the region. Under scenario one, \na similar pattern is expected to what has been observed over the \npast few days. If scenario two plays out, the hot and dry weather \npattern continues. Details will become clearer as we get closer to\ntime. \n\nLamberson",
